{
  "id": "the-closed-port",
  "name": "The Closed Port",
  "category": "security",
  "difficulty": 2,
  "xp": 200,
  "level": 8,
  "description": "The server is up, and refuses the connection anyway.",
  "brief": "The security team asked for the site to be served over HTTPS. Right now port 443 refuses every connection, while port 80 answers happily.\n\nNote the word refuses. The server is reachable and answering — it is saying no. That is a very different fault from silence.\n\nUse the Connection Test panel to see both, then enable HTTPS on WEB-01.",
  "hints": [
    "Test PC-01 → web.netquest.local on port 80, then on 443. Compare.",
    "'Refused' means the host answered. Something has to be listening.",
    "Run 'netstat' on WEB-01 to see which ports are actually open.",
    "Enable HTTPS in WEB-01's Services list."
  ],
  "explanation": "A refused connection is an answer. The host received the request, found nothing listening on that port, and replied with a reset — which is why the result came back instantly.\n\nContrast that with a firewall drop, where nothing comes back at all. Refused means look at the server; filtered means look at what is in between. Telling those two apart saves an enormous amount of time.",
